<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Section 3.4 - Part 2 - Determinants using Cofactor Expansion</title>
 
<page>
  

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Using a cofactor expansion, show the following:
	<md> \det \begin{bmatrix} 
   2\amp0 \amp-1\\
	 3 \amp1 \amp 2 \\
	-2 \amp4 \amp-3
	\end{bmatrix} = -36. </md>
You can choose any row or column to create the expansion, but the choice of a particular row or column may simplify the computation.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="20-solutions">
      <p>
        Using a cofactor expansion along the second column, we have:
        <md>
        <mrow>  \det(A) = 0 + 
          1 \cdot \det\left(\begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ -2\amp -3 \end{bmatrix}\right) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp + 
          4 \cdot \det\left(\begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ 3 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}\right). </mrow>
        </md>
      Using that the determinant of a 2x2 matrix <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>
      is given by <m>ad - bc</m>, we have:
        <md>
         <mrow> \det(A) \amp = 0 + (-1)^{2+2} \cdot 1 \cdot (2(-3) - (-1)(-2)) + (-1)^{3+2}\cdot 4 \cdot (2(2) - (-1)(3)) </mrow> 
         <mrow> = -6 + 4(7) = -8 + -28 = -36. </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</page>

<page>
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the determinant of the matrix using a cofactor expansion:
<md>
A =
\left[
  \begin{array}{ccc}
    2 \amp 1 \amp -3\\
    1 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\
    -2\amp 4 \amp 1
  \end{array}
\right].
</md>  
  (You can check your work with Sage.)</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="20-solutions">
      <p>
        Using a co-factor expansion along the third column, we have:
        <md>
          <mrow> \det(A)= (-1)^{1+3}(-3) \amp \det\left(\begin{bmatrix}2 \amp 1 \\ 1\amp 4 \end{bmatrix}\right)  </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp + 0 </mrow>
           <mrow> \amp + (-1)^{3+3} (1)\det\left(\begin{bmatrix}2 \amp 1 \\ 1\amp 4 \end{bmatrix}\right) </mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp = (1)(-3)(8-1) + (1)(1)(8-1) </mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp = -21 + 7 = -14. </mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </page>

<page>
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Use a cofactor expansion to find the determinant of 
<md> \det \begin{bmatrix} 
	-3 \amp0 \amp 0 \amp0\\
	 4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp0\\
	-1 \amp4 \amp-4 \amp0\\
	 0 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 3
	\end{bmatrix}. </md>
	Explain how the cofactor expansion technique shows that the determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of its diagonal entries.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="20-solutions">
      <p>
        Starting with a cofactor expansion along the first row, we have:
        <md>
          (-1)^{1+1}(-3) \det\left( \begin{bmatrix} 
          
	1 \amp 0 \amp0\\
	4 \amp-4 \amp0\\
	 3 \amp 2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} \right) + 0 + 0 + 0.
        </md>
        which simplifies to 
        <md>
          -3 \det\left( \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp0\\
	4 \amp-4 \amp0\\
	 3 \amp 2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} \right) 
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we can do a cofactor expansion along the first row of the resulting 3x3 matrix (keeping track of the <m>-3</m> factor 
        from the previous step):
        <md>
          (-3)(-1)^{1+1}(1) \det\left( \begin{bmatrix} 
          -4 \amp0\\
	         2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} \right) + 0 + 0.
        </md>
        which simplifies to
        <md>
          (-3)(1)\det\left( \begin{bmatrix} 
          -4 \amp0\\
	         2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} \right)
        </md>
        
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, we can compute the determinant of the resulting 2x2 matrix:
        <md>
         (-3)(1)((-4)(3) - (0)(2)) = (-3)(1)(-12) = 36.
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </page>

